    {
      "id": "s06",
      "type": "CODE_RUN",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Now, the real lines. The first one's the plain call. Hand Playwright a path, you get a png back. The second adds full page equals true, and that one little argument changes the file completely. The third hangs the very same call off a locator, instead of off the page. Small difference. Huge result.",
      "data": {
        "codeRun": {
          "filename": "shots.py",
          "language": "py",
          "resultLabel": "what lands on disk",
          "color": "blue",
          "atWord": 2.75,
          "caption": "the same verb, three different subjects",
          "lines": [
            {
              "text": "page.screenshot(path=\"now.png\")",
              "detail": "what is on screen right now",
              "sub": "now.png, one screenful",
              "label": "file",
              "atWord": 9.583
            },
            {
              "text": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "page.screenshot(path=\"whole.png\",",
              "detail": "the whole scrolled page",
              "sub": "whole.png, top to bottom",
              "label": "file",
              "atWord": 21.583
            },
            {
              "text": "                full_page=True)"
            },
            {
              "text": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "page.get_by_test_id(\"chart\")",
              "detail": "off a locator, not the page",
              "sub": "chart.png, one element",
              "label": "file",
              "atWord": 34.5
            },
            {
              "text": "    .screenshot(path=\"chart.png\")"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 578,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-13-evidence_long_s06.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s07",
      "type": "FLAG_HARVEST",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "But here's the move nobody tells beginners about, and you don't call screenshot for any of it. You add one flag to the command you're already running. That's the whole change. Now every test that fails drops a photo on its own, and the ones that pass leave you absolutely nothing. Look at the gap between those rows. Not one line of code went into your test file.",
      "data": {
        "flagHarvest": {
          "flag": "--screenshot only-on-failure",
          "command": "pytest",
          "folder": "test-results/",
          "quietNote": "nothing kept",
          "artifacts": [
            "test-failed-1.png"
          ],
          "caption": "zero code. evidence only where it failed.",
          "color": "orange",
          "atWord": 2.5,
          "harvestAtWord": 35.417,
          "tests": [
            {
